# Aggregation in SQL

## Definition

**Aggregation** is the process of performing a calculation on a set of values and returning a single summary value. Aggregate functions operate on multiple rows and return one result per group (or one result for the entire table if no grouping is specified).

> **Key Rule:** Aggregate functions ignore `NULL` values (except `COUNT(*)`).

---

## Aggregate Functions — Quick Reference

| Function | Syntax | Description |
|----------|--------|-------------|
| `COUNT` | `COUNT(col)` / `COUNT(*)` | Counts rows; `COUNT(*)` includes NULLs, `COUNT(col)` excludes them |
| `SUM` | `SUM(col)` | Sum of all non-NULL values |
| `AVG` | `AVG(col)` | Average of all non-NULL values |
| `MAX` | `MAX(col)` | Maximum value in the column |
| `MIN` | `MIN(col)` | Minimum value in the column |

---

## Sample Table

```sql
CREATE TABLE Employee (
    emp_id    INT,
    name      VARCHAR(50),
    dept      VARCHAR(30),
    salary    DECIMAL(10,2),
    manager   VARCHAR(50)
);
```

| emp_id | name    | dept    | salary  | manager |
|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|
| 1      | Alice   | HR      | 60000   | NULL    |
| 2      | Bob     | IT      | 80000   | Alice   |
| 3      | Carol   | IT      | 90000   | Alice   |
| 4      | Dave    | HR      | 55000   | Alice   |
| 5      | Eve     | Finance | 70000   | Bob     |
| 6      | Frank   | Finance | 75000   | Bob     |

